1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:04,740
Welcome to chapter 12. This chapter deals with kidney and urinary tract pathology.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:10,780
We're going to begin with congenital lesions. These are relatively high yield because they

3
00:00:10,780 --> 00:00:16,380
give us a chance to tie a little bit of embryology in with organ systems. And of course, that's

4
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:21,480
really a great way for examiners to go after embryology type questions in the context of

5
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:27,380
clinical scenarios. So the first disorder that we want to be familiar with is called a horseshoe

6
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:32,880
kidney. Now the idea here is that we get a conjoined kidney, usually at the lower pole,

7
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:38,540
and this is the most common congenital renal anomaly. Here's a picture of a horseshoe kidney,

8
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:44,340
and you can clearly see that this kidney obviously has the shape of a horseshoe. What is important,

9
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:49,740
however, is that this kidney is usually going to be located abnormally in the lower abdomen.

10
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:55,620
Now this becomes important because recall from embryology that the kidney is actually developed

11
00:00:55,620 --> 00:01:01,640
in the pelvis, and then it ascends into the abdomen. Now imagine that as this kidney is trying to

12
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:07,640
ascend into the abdomen, it's going to get stuck on the root of the inferior mesenteric artery,

13
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:14,160
and so as it's trying to go up and it gets stuck on the root of the IMA, its ascension is going to be

14
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:20,080
blocked, and so this kidney will be abnormally located in the abdomen. The next congenital disorder

15
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:25,880
is renal agenesis. A means without, and genesis means production, so in this particular case there's an

16
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:31,100
absence of kidney formation. Now that absence of kidney formation can be unilateral or it can be

17
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:37,260
bilateral. In unilateral agenesis, the complication will be that the patient will be lacking one kidney,

18
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:44,540
and the consequence of that will be that the existing kidney will undergo hypertrophy. Now the existing

19
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:48,360
kidney, when it undergoes hypertrophy, is actually going to be doing the work of two kidneys.

20
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:54,760
That's not really a problem until many years down the line, and that's when hyperfiltration injury

21
00:01:54,760 --> 00:02:02,580
will begin to lead to a risk of renal failure. In bilateral renal agenesis, there is lack of

22
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:07,880
formation of both kidneys, and if I had to summarize this in one word, I would summarize it with

23
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:14,460
oligohydramnios. Recall from embryology that the baby floats around in amniotic fluid, and that

24
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:20,060
amniotic fluid essentially is the baby's filtrate coming from the kidney, i.e. the baby's floating

25
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:26,740
around in its own urine. The consequence of having low amniotic fluid are multiple. Number one, the

26
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:32,720
fetus will develop lung hypoplasia. Go back and remind yourselves that when the lung develops

27
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:39,980
embryologically, one way by which it grows is by stretch, and so as the baby is in the womb, it's breathing

28
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:46,120
in and out amniotic fluid, and the breathing in of amniotic fluid stretches the lung, and when the

29
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:51,760
baby breathes out, that again causes collapse of the lung, and it's the stretching and the collapse

30
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:57,000
of the lung that actually allows for development of the lung to occur. So you can imagine that if there

31
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:02,680
isn't enough amniotic fluid, the lung will not be expanded appropriately, and therefore the lung will

32
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:08,880
undergo hypoplasia, and so one consequence of having bilateral renal agenesis is lung hypoplasia.

33
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:14,420
Another consequence is that there will be a flat face with low-set ears, and the way I think about this is

34
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:19,960
that normally the baby's in a bubble, and it's floating around in amniotic fluid. However, if the amniotic fluid

35
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:26,160
is low or lacking, then the face will get pressed up against the wall of the uterus, and if the face gets pressed

36
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:31,800
up against the wall of the uterus, for example, then that will result in a flat face with low-set ears.

37
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:38,500
And finally, there can also be a risk of developmental defects of the extremities. Same consequence, the extremities

38
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:43,580
normally require that bubble of amniotic fluid to develop properly, but here they're going to be

39
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:48,480
pressed up against mom's tissue, and when they're pressed up against mom, that's going to result in

40
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:56,580
developmental defects of the extremities. This entire sequence, hypoplasia of the lung, flat face with

41
00:03:56,580 --> 00:04:01,700
low-set ears, and developmental defects of the extremities, this whole sequence is called Potter sequence,

42
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:06,780
and that's something to be familiar with. Again, it goes back to embryology. Bilateral renal

43
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:12,780
agenesis is incompatible with life. However, when an examination of the fetus was done, we'd see the

44
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:19,140
classic findings of Potter sequence, very high yield because it ties into embryology. Here's a picture of

45
00:04:19,140 --> 00:04:25,320
a fetus with Potter sequence. There's flattening of the face with low-set ears, and then some extremity

46
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:30,880
defects as well. Again, high yield to remember that these are all a consequence of oligohydramnios.

47
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:37,080
The next of the congenital defects is actually going to be kicking off a little bit of a theme.

48
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:43,020
Now, what we want to discuss as we move forward are some congenital defects that result in cyst

49
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:48,680
formation in the kidney, and there's a few of them. The first of them is dysplastic kidney. The idea

50
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:54,140
here is that you have a non-inherited, and that's important, congenital malformation of the

51
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:59,520
renal parenchyma that's characterized by cysts with abnormal tissue. Now, basically, these patients

52
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:05,580
develop cysts within their kidney. However, this is not inherited. The cysts are characterized by

53
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:12,400
abnormal tissue, especially cartilage. Here's a picture of an example where we've got a cystic

54
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:19,920
space, and that cystic space is lined by a wall, and within that wall, we can clearly see that there

55
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:26,800
is cartilage. Dysplastic kidney is actually not inherited, and that's important because if a fetus

56
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:34,360
has dysplastic kidney, and we asked the question, what's the risk of developing a similar dysplastic

57
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:43,140
kidney in a subsequent pregnancy, the risk would be relatively low because, again, this is not an inherited

58
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:49,660
disorder. Now, clinically, this is usually unilateral. However, on examinations, it's usually

59
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:54,800
bilateral, and the reason it's usually bilateral on examinations is because they like to try to confuse

60
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:59,540
you with inherited polycystic kidney disease. I think every medical student, when they hear about

61
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:03,980
cysts in the kidney, the first thing that comes to their mind is polycystic kidney disease. However,

62
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:08,140
it is important to note that there are other diseases that can also cause cysts in the kidney,

63
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:12,940
and so examiners like to go after them to see if they can get you to knee-jerk and select

64
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:18,680
polycystic kidney disease to see whether you really understand the spectrum of what can happen

65
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:25,020
within the kidney. The next of the cystic diseases of the kidney is polycystic kidney disease,

66
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:32,740
also known as PKD. This is an inherited defect, and it always results in bilateral enlargement of the

67
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:38,460
kidneys. The reason that the kidneys are enlarged is that the patients get cysts in both the renal cortex

68
00:06:38,460 --> 00:06:46,500
and the medulla, and that's important to note. Again, inherited defect, bilateral, with cysts in the

69
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:52,840
renal cortex and the medulla. Now, when you look at a picture of polycystic kidney disease, you clearly see

70
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:59,640
that there is cyst formation and that the kidney is enlarged. So both kidneys, again, will be enlarged.

71
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:05,260
Now, polycystic kidney disease can actually be divided into two subtypes. There's an autosomal recessive form

72
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:11,560
and an autosomal dominant form. The autosomal recessive form classically used to be called the juvenile form,

73
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:17,560
and that's because it usually presents in infants. It presents with renal failure and hypertension,

74
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:24,700
and newborns can present with the Potter sequence, very high yield. Imagine if a patient has autosomal

75
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:30,500
recessive polycystic kidney disease, and it's so severe that the kidney isn't functioning. It's as if

76
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:36,120
they have bilateral renal agenesis. Again, the kidneys would not be producing fluid, and hence there would be

77
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:41,240
oligohydramnios with all of the resultant consequences of oligohydramnios, i.e. Potter

78
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:47,480
sequence. The associations of polycystic kidney disease are also important to know, and in an autosomal

79
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:55,380
recessive form, it'll be associated with congenital hepatic fibrosis and hepatic cysts. The way I think

80
00:07:55,380 --> 00:08:00,560
about this is that the patients get cysts in the kidney and cysts in the liver, and that the cysts in

81
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:06,400
the liver can result in congenital hepatic fibrosis. The presenting sign of this congenital hepatic

82
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:11,840
fibrosis will be that the patients will have signs and symptoms of portal hypertension. So when you have

83
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:18,000
a baby with signs and symptoms of portal hypertension, think about autosomal recessive polycystic kidney

84
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:24,800
disease. The additional form of polycystic kidney disease is autosomal dominant form, and the autosomal

85
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:33,200
dominant form presents in young adults. Some people like to use the mnemonic ADULT. So the AD is

86
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:38,800
autosomal dominant, and it's also the first two letters of adult. So this presents in young adults.

87
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:45,840
Now, the defect is actually present in the fetus at the time of birth, so it's due to a mutation in

88
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:52,960
adult polycystic kidney disease gene 1 or adult polycystic kidney disease gene 2. But the cysts,

89
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:58,800
they develop over time. And since the cysts develop over time, these patients will present

90
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:05,840
as adults. The way by which they present is hypertension, hematuria, and worsening renal failure.

91
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:11,360
The hypertension is usually due to an increased renin, so that these patients will also have an

92
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:18,560
increased plasma renin. There are some associations to be aware of in this particular form of polycystic

93
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:25,840
kidney disease, and they include bary aneurysms, hepatic cysts, and mitral valve prolapse. And the

94
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:31,360
bary aneurysms are particularly important because they are a cause of death in this particular subgroup

95
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:36,720
of patients. So if you have a clinical vignette, for example, in which you're told that there is a

96
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:43,280
family history of renal disease, and there is death occurring because of renal disease or brain

97
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:48,400
hemorrhage in, for example, the grandfather or the great grandfather, then you really want to put two

98
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:53,680
and two together and think about autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. The way I have my

99
00:09:53,680 --> 00:10:00,320
students remember this is that they get cysts in the liver, cysts in the brain, and cysts in the kidney.

100
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:06,240
Now, what do I mean by that? Well, the cysts in the kidney are the cysts of polycystic kidney disease.

101
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:12,400
The cysts in the brain is a cystic dilatation of a blood vessel in the brain. And remember that

102
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:19,760
bary aneurysms are basically balloon-like dilatations of a blood vessel in the brain. So you can think

103
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:25,120
about it as quote unquote a cyst. Additionally, they get hepatic cysts or cysts in the liver.

104
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:30,000
The last of the associations to be familiar with is mitral valve prolapse, and these patients can also

105
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,360
have mitral valve prolapse. And again, mitral valve prolapse can rarely be a cause of death as well.

106
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:41,760
The final of the cystic diseases and congenital diseases that we want to discuss is medullary

107
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:46,480
cystic kidney disease. Now, what's important here is, again, this is an inherited defect.

108
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:54,240
There are cysts in the kidney. However, the cysts are in the medullary collecting ducts of the kidney,

109
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:59,760
hence the name medullary cystic kidney disease. So that's one key distinction from polycystic kidney

110
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:05,040
disease is that here the cysts would be in the medullary collecting ducts. A second important

111
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:10,480
distinction from polycystic kidney disease is the fact that these patients have shrunken kidneys.

112
00:11:11,280 --> 00:11:15,840
Now remember that in polycystic kidney disease, whether it be adult form or juvenile form,

113
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:22,080
the kidneys will be enlarged. However, in medullary cystic kidney disease, the kidneys are shrunken

114
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:29,360
and the patients will present with worsening renal failure. So again, shrunken kidneys with cysts in

115
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:34,880
the medulla of the kidney. And that concludes our congenital section. We're now going to move on to

116
00:11:34,880 --> 00:11:37,600
acute renal failure.

